## The Three Workflows Explained

### **Workflow 1: plan.yml (Pull Request Validator)**

**When it runs:**
- Every time you push code to a branch with an open PR
- Only if files in `environments/prod/` or `modules/` changed

**What it does:**

```
Step 1: Checkout Code
└─ Downloads your code from GitHub
   Purpose: Actions runner needs your terraform files

Step 2: Configure AWS Credentials (OIDC)
└─ Logs into AWS using OIDC (secure, no passwords stored)
   Purpose: Terraform needs AWS permission to read current state

Step 3: Setup Terraform
└─ Installs terraform binary on the runner
   Purpose: Terraform isn't pre-installed

Step 4: Terraform Format Check
└─ Checks if code follows HCL formatting standards
   Purpose: Catch style issues early

Step 5: Terraform Init
└─ Downloads modules, configures S3 backend, loads plugins
   Purpose: Terraform needs to know about your infrastructure

Step 6: Terraform Validate
└─ Checks terraform syntax (catches typos, wrong references)
   Purpose: Prevents syntax errors from reaching production

Step 7: Terraform Plan
└─ Creates a preview of what will change in AWS
   Purpose: Show reviewers exactly what will happen

Step 8: Comment PR with Plan
└─ Posts the terraform plan as a GitHub comment
   Purpose: Reviewers see changes without leaving GitHub

Step 9: Fail if plan failed
└─ Blocks the PR if plan errored
   Purpose: Prevent bad code from being merged
```

**Example output on PR:**
```
## 📋 Terraform Plan
Status: success

<details>
<summary>Click to expand plan output</summary>

# aws_instance.web will be created
+ resource "aws_instance" "web" {
    + ami           = "ami-0d7405d05f836d0d4"
    + instance_type = "t3.micro"
    + ...
}

No changes. Infrastructure matches configuration.
</details>
```

**Why this matters:**
- ✅ Prevents bad code from being merged
- ✅ Shows what will change before it changes
- ✅ Catches configuration errors early
- ✅ Provides visibility to reviewers

